ENG.add('h1', '독해: 주제·제목·요지', {
  sub: '글 전체를 아우르는 핵심 찾기',
  kw: ['주제', '제목', '요지', '주제문', '두괄식·미괄식', '통념-반박', '신호어', '오답 함정'],
  sections: [
    { h: '글의 핵심을 담은 주제문',
      p: `주제·제목·요지 문제는 모두 글 전체를 아우르는 <span class="hl">주제문</span>을 찾는 문제이다. 주제문은 구체적인 예시나 근거가 아니라 그것들을 묶는 <b>일반적인 진술</b>이며, 대개 글의 처음이나 끝에 온다. 통념을 먼저 소개하고 However 뒤에서 뒤집는 글은 반박 부분이 주제문이다.`,
      table: { head: ['글의 구조', '주제문 위치', '특징'],
        rows: [['두괄식', '첫 부분', '주장 → 예시·근거'],
               ['미괄식', '마지막 부분', '예시·근거 → So/Therefore + 결론'],
               ['양괄식', '처음과 끝', '처음 주장을 끝에서 다른 말로 다시 말한다'],
               ['통념-반박', 'However/But 뒤', '많은 사람의 생각 → 그러나 실제로는 …']],
        cap: '주제문이 오는 자리' },
      ex: [[`Many people think breakfast is not important. However, it gives the brain the energy it needs to start the day.`, '많은 사람이 아침 식사가 중요하지 않다고 생각한다. 그러나 아침은 뇌가 하루를 시작하는 데 필요한 에너지를 준다.'],
           [`For example, students who eat breakfast pay better attention in class.`, '예를 들어, 아침을 먹는 학생들은 수업에 더 집중한다.']],
      tip: 'For example 바로 앞 문장은 주제문일 가능성이 높다. 예시는 주제문을 뒷받침하기 위해 나온다.' },
    { h: '유형별 풀이 순서',
      p: `세 유형은 묻는 방식만 다를 뿐 풀이 순서는 같다. <b>첫 2~3문장에서 소재</b>를 잡고, 신호어 뒤에서 <b>글쓴이의 생각</b>을 확인한 뒤, 그 생각을 가장 정확하게 담은 보기를 고른다.`,
      table: { head: ['유형', '보기 형태', '고르는 기준'],
        rows: [['주제', '영어 명사구 (the role of …)', '글 전체 내용을 빠짐없이, 넘치지 않게 담은 것'],
               ['제목', '영어 구·문장, 의문문·비유', '주제를 담되 비유·의문문으로 바꾸어 표현한 것'],
               ['요지', '우리말 문장', '글쓴이의 주장·결론 (should, must 문장)']],
        cap: '공통 순서: 소재 파악 → 신호어 찾기 → 주제문 확인 → 보기와 비교' },
      p2: '제목 보기는 <b>비유적 표현</b>(Natural Air Conditioner 등)이나 의문문으로 나오는 경우가 많다. 겉모양에 속지 말고 그 표현이 무엇을 뜻하는지 우리말로 바꿔 보자.' },
    { h: '주제문을 알려 주는 신호어',
      p: `글쓴이는 중요한 말을 하기 전에 <span class="hl">신호어</span>를 쓴다. 역접·결론·당위의 표현 뒤에는 주제문이, 예시·연구 표현 뒤에는 근거가 오는 경우가 많다.`,
      table: { head: ['신호어', '예', '뒤에 오는 것'],
        rows: [['역접', 'However, But, Yet, In fact', '통념을 뒤집는 글쓴이의 주장'],
               ['결론', 'Therefore, Thus, In short, In conclusion', '글 전체의 결론(요지)'],
               ['당위·강조', 'should, must, need to, It is important to', '글쓴이가 하고 싶은 말'],
               ['재진술', 'In other words, That is', '앞 내용을 쉽게 다시 말한 핵심'],
               ['예시·근거', 'For example, Studies show …', '주제문을 뒷받침하는 세부 내용']] },
      ex: [[`In short, small habits lead to big changes.`, '요컨대, 작은 습관이 큰 변화로 이어진다.'],
           [`Studies show that people remember more after a short rest.`, '연구에 따르면 사람들은 잠깐 쉰 뒤에 더 많이 기억한다.']] },
    { h: '오답 함정 피하기',
      p: `주제·제목·요지 문제의 오답은 대부분 <b>지문 속 단어를 그대로 쓰면서</b> 범위나 방향을 살짝 바꾼 것이다. 보기를 고른 뒤에는 “이 보기가 글 전체를 설명하는가?”를 꼭 확인한다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정', '설명', '예 (주제: 나무가 도시를 시원하게 한다)'],
        rows: [['너무 넓음', '글보다 훨씬 큰 범위', 'the importance of nature'],
               ['너무 좁음', '예시 하나만 다룸', 'how leaves block sunlight'],
               ['단어 재활용', '지문 단어를 섞어 다른 뜻을 만듦', 'how asphalt is made'],
               ['반대 방향', '글쓴이 생각과 반대', 'the dangers of planting trees'],
               ['상식만 맞음', '옳은 말이지만 글에 없음', 'Parks are good for health.']] },
      warn: '지문에 많이 나온 단어가 들어 있다고 정답이 아니다. 오히려 그런 보기가 함정인 경우가 많다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: '다음 중 바로 뒤에 글쓴이의 결론(요지)이 나올 가능성이 가장 높은 신호어는?', o: ['For example', 'In addition', 'Therefore', 'For instance'], a: 2,
      why: 'Therefore(그러므로)는 앞 내용을 정리해 결론을 이끈다. For example·For instance는 예시, In addition은 내용을 덧붙일 때 쓴다.' },
    { lv: 1, q: '다음 글에서 주제문은?<br>① Exercise does more than make your body strong. ② It reduces stress and helps you feel calm. ③ It also helps you sleep better at night. ④ Even a short walk can improve your mood.', o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 0,
      why: '①은 ‘운동은 몸을 튼튼하게 하는 것 이상의 일을 한다’는 일반적 진술이고, ②~④는 그 구체적인 예(스트레스, 수면, 기분)이다. 두괄식 글이다.' },
    { lv: 1, q: '다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<br>Many teens use their phones right before bed. The blue light from screens tells the brain that it is still daytime. As a result, they find it harder to fall asleep.', o: ['the history of smartphones', 'how screen use before bed affects sleep', 'the importance of daylight for plants', 'why teens need eight hours of sleep'], a: 1,
      why: '자기 전 화면의 빛이 잠들기 어렵게 만든다는 내용이다. 스마트폰의 역사는 너무 넓고, 8시간 수면은 글에 나오지 않는다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Most of us try hard to avoid boredom. We check our phones in line, listen to music while walking, and fill every free minute with something to do. However, boredom may not be as useless as we think. When the mind has nothing to focus on, it starts to wander, and this wandering often leads to new ideas. In one experiment, people who first did a dull task, such as copying numbers from a list, later came up with more creative uses for a plastic cup than people who had not been bored. Feeling bored pushes us to search for something more interesting, even inside our own heads. So the next time you feel bored, try not to reach for your phone right away. Your best idea might be waiting on the other side of boredom.</div>`,
      o: ['ways to avoid boredom in daily life', 'the negative effects of smartphones on teens', 'the importance of finishing dull tasks quickly', 'the positive role of boredom in creative thinking'], a: 3,
      why: 'However 뒤에서 지루함이 쓸모없지 않고 새로운 생각으로 이어진다고 주장한다. ①은 글쓴이 생각과 반대이고, ②는 스마트폰이라는 일부 소재만 다룬다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When children do well on a test, many parents say, "You're so smart!" This sounds like kind encouragement, but it can have an unexpected effect. Children who are praised for being smart often start to believe that intelligence is something they either have or don't have. As a result, they avoid difficult challenges, because failing might show that they are not smart after all. On the other hand, children who are praised for their effort, as in "You worked really hard on this," learn that they can improve by trying. These children are more willing to take on hard problems and keep going after mistakes. Therefore, if parents want their children to grow, they should praise the process, not the ability.</div>`,
      o: ['아이의 지능을 높이려면 어려운 문제를 많이 풀게 해야 한다.', '아이가 실수를 하면 즉시 바로잡아 주어야 한다.', '아이를 칭찬할 때는 능력보다 노력과 과정을 칭찬해야 한다.', '부모는 아이의 시험 결과에 지나치게 관심을 두지 말아야 한다.'], a: 2,
      why: 'Therefore 뒤의 마지막 문장 ‘능력이 아니라 과정을 칭찬해야 한다’가 요지이다. 어려운 문제나 실수 교정은 글의 일부 단어만 빌린 오답이다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">On a hot summer afternoon, the temperature in a city park can be several degrees lower than on a nearby street. The reason is trees. Their leaves block sunlight before it reaches the ground, and they release water into the air, which cools the surroundings much like sweat cools our skin. Streets without trees, by contrast, are covered with asphalt and concrete that absorb heat during the day and release it at night. This is why many large cities are now planting thousands of new trees along roads and on empty lots. Some cities even use maps of surface temperatures to decide where trees are needed most. Planting trees may seem like a small step, but it is one of the cheapest ways to keep a city cool.</div>`,
      o: ["Trees: A City's Natural Air Conditioner", 'Why Parks Are Becoming Smaller', 'How Asphalt Is Made from Oil', 'The Dangers of Planting Trees Near Roads'], a: 0,
      why: '나무가 그늘과 수분 방출로 도시를 시원하게 한다는 글이므로, 나무를 ‘자연 에어컨’에 비유한 제목이 알맞다. asphalt는 지문 단어를 재활용한 함정이다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Many language learners stay silent in class because they are afraid of making mistakes. They wait until they can produce perfect sentences before they speak. Unfortunately, that day rarely comes. Language is a skill, like swimming or riding a bike, and skills develop through practice, not through waiting. Every time learners try to say something and get it slightly wrong, they receive feedback, either from a teacher's correction or from a listener's confused face. This feedback shows them exactly what they need to fix. Learners who speak often, even with errors, therefore improve much faster than those who only listen. In this sense, mistakes are not a sign of failure but a necessary part of the learning process.</div>`,
      o: ['the difficulty of learning to swim as an adult', 'the role of mistakes in improving language skills', 'the importance of perfect grammar in writing', "why teachers should not correct students' errors"], a: 1,
      why: '마지막 문장처럼 실수는 언어 학습에 꼭 필요한 과정이라는 내용이다. 수영은 비유로 든 예일 뿐이고, 교사의 교정은 오히려 도움이 되는 피드백으로 소개되었다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Scrolling through social media, you see a friend on a beach vacation, another holding a prize, and a classmate at a fancy restaurant. Suddenly your own ordinary weekend feels dull. This feeling is common, but it is based on a false comparison. People usually post only the best moments of their lives: the delicious meals, not the burnt ones; the trophies, not the lost games. Meanwhile, you know every detail of your own life, including the boring and difficult parts. In other words, you are comparing your whole life to the highlights of other people's lives. To stay satisfied with your own life, remember that what you see online is a carefully chosen picture, not the full story.</div>`,
      o: ['SNS 사용 시간을 줄여야 학업에 집중할 수 있다.', '친구의 성공을 진심으로 축하해 주어야 한다.', '평범한 주말에도 특별한 활동을 계획해야 한다.', 'SNS 속 타인의 모습은 일부일 뿐이므로 자신의 삶과 비교하지 말아야 한다.'], a: 3,
      why: 'In other words 문장과 마지막 문장에서, SNS에는 남의 가장 좋은 순간만 보이므로 내 삶 전체와 비교하는 것은 잘못이라고 말한다. 사용 시간이나 학업은 글에 없다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When a honeybee finds a good patch of flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a special movement known as the waggle dance. The bee runs in a straight line while shaking its body, then circles back and repeats the pattern. The direction of the straight run shows where the flowers are compared to the position of the sun. The length of the run tells how far away they are: the longer the waggle, the farther the flowers. Other bees watch the dance closely and then fly directly to the food source. Through this simple performance, a single bee can share detailed information with thousands of its sisters. In a way, bees have a language written not in words but in movement.</div>`,
      o: ['How Bees Make Honey from Flowers', 'The Sun: A Danger to Honeybees', 'Dancing Directions: How Bees Share Information', 'Why Bees Live in Large Colonies'], a: 2,
      why: '벌이 춤으로 꽃의 방향과 거리를 동료에게 알린다는 글이다. 꿀을 만드는 법이나 무리 생활의 이유는 다루지 않았고, 해는 방향의 기준일 뿐 위험이 아니다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Many people are proud of their ability to multitask. They answer messages while watching a lecture or do homework with the TV on, believing they are saving time. However, research on the brain tells a different story. The brain cannot actually focus on two demanding tasks at once; instead, it switches rapidly between them. Each switch has a small cost, as the brain must stop one task, remember where it was in the other, and restart. These costs add up quickly. In some studies, people who switched between tasks took up to 40 percent longer and made more errors than those who finished one task before starting the next. What feels like efficiency is often just a busy way of working slowly.</div>`,
      o: ['the hidden costs of switching between tasks', 'the benefits of watching lectures online', 'how to answer messages more quickly', 'the growing number of tasks students face'], a: 0,
      why: '통념(멀티태스킹은 시간을 아낀다)을 However 뒤에서 뒤집는 글로, 작업 전환에 숨은 비용이 있다는 것이 주제이다. 나머지는 예시 속 단어를 빌린 오답이다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When people decide to change their lives, they often set huge goals: run every morning, read a book a week, study three hours a day. After a few days, most of these plans fall apart. The problem is not a lack of motivation but the size of the goal. A big change requires a lot of energy, and on a tired or busy day, that energy simply isn't there. A better approach is to start with a habit so small that it feels almost too easy, such as putting on your running shoes or reading one page. Once the small action becomes automatic, it is much easier to build on it. Lasting change comes not from dramatic plans but from tiny steps repeated every day.</div>`,
      o: ['목표를 높게 세워야 동기가 강해진다.', '습관을 바꾸려면 아주 작은 행동부터 꾸준히 반복해야 한다.', '운동과 독서를 함께 해야 효과가 크다.', '피곤한 날에는 계획을 미루고 충분히 쉬어야 한다.'], a: 1,
      why: '마지막 문장 ‘지속적인 변화는 극적인 계획이 아니라 매일 반복하는 작은 걸음에서 온다’가 요지이다. 큰 목표는 글쓴이가 문제로 지적한 것이다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Modern concrete structures often begin to crack within a few decades, especially near the sea. Yet some harbor walls built by the ancient Romans about 2,000 years ago are still standing today. Scientists have long wondered why. Recent studies suggest that the secret lies in the ingredients. The Romans mixed lime with volcanic ash, and when seawater soaked into this mixture, it caused slow chemical reactions that formed new minerals. These minerals filled tiny cracks and made the material even stronger over time. In other words, while seawater damages modern concrete, it actually helped Roman concrete grow tougher. Engineers are now studying this ancient recipe to create longer-lasting buildings for the future.</div>`,
      o: ['Why Modern Buildings Are Safer Than Ancient Ones', 'The Rise and Fall of the Roman Empire', 'How Volcanoes Destroyed Roman Harbors', 'An Ancient Recipe That Gets Stronger with Time'], a: 3,
      why: '바닷물과 반응해 시간이 갈수록 더 단단해진 로마 콘크리트의 비밀을 다룬 글이다. 로마 제국의 흥망은 너무 넓고, 화산은 재료(화산재)로 나왔을 뿐 항구를 파괴하지 않았다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Today's children often have schedules as busy as those of adults. After school, they move from piano lessons to soccer practice to math tutoring, with every hour planned by grown-ups. These activities certainly teach useful skills. However, something important may be lost when there is no time left for free, unstructured play. When children make up their own games, they must invent the rules, settle arguments, and decide what to do when things go wrong. No coach or teacher is there to solve problems for them. Through this process, they develop creativity, independence, and the ability to get along with others, skills that are hard to teach in a classroom. Leaving some empty space in a child's schedule, then, is not a waste of time but an investment in growth.</div>`,
      o: ['the need for more after-school lessons', "the role of coaches in children's sports", "the value of free play in children's development", 'the dangers of playing outdoors without adults'], a: 2,
      why: '바쁜 일정(통념)을 소개한 뒤 However 이하에서 아이들끼리 하는 자유 놀이가 창의성·독립성·사회성을 길러 준다고 주장한다. 방과 후 수업을 늘리자는 것은 반대 방향이다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We tend to think of listening as a passive activity: the speaker does the work while the listener simply receives the words. But good listening is far more active than it looks. A good listener pays attention not only to the words but also to the speaker's tone and expressions. He or she asks questions to clarify meaning and resists the urge to plan a reply while the other person is still talking. Most of all, a good listener holds back judgment long enough to understand the speaker's point of view. This takes real effort and self-control. It is no surprise, then, that people who feel truly heard are more likely to trust the listener. Listening well is a skill that must be practiced, not a talent we are simply born with.</div>`,
      o: ['잘 듣는 것은 노력과 연습이 필요한 능동적인 활동이다.', '대화에서는 말하는 사람이 더 많은 책임을 져야 한다.', '상대의 말을 들으면서 대답을 미리 준비해야 한다.', '사람의 말투보다 내용에 집중해야 오해를 줄일 수 있다.'], a: 0,
      why: 'But 이하와 마지막 문장에서 듣기는 노력이 필요한 능동적 기술이라고 말한다. 대답을 미리 준비하는 것은 좋은 청자가 참는 행동이고, 말투에도 주의해야 한다고 했으므로 ④도 틀리다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Have you ever had a song stuck in your head because you heard only part of it? Psychologists have found that our minds tend to hold on to unfinished tasks more strongly than finished ones. In a well-known observation, a waiter could remember every detail of orders that had not yet been paid for, but forgot them almost as soon as the customers paid and left. Once a task is complete, the brain seems to release it from memory. Until then, it keeps reminding us. This tendency can be stressful, but it can also be useful. Some writers stop working in the middle of a sentence so that they will feel a strong urge to return the next day. An unfinished task, it seems, has a power that a finished one lacks.</div>`,
      o: ['Why We Forget Songs So Easily', 'The Pull of the Unfinished', 'How Waiters Train Their Memory', 'Finish What You Start, or Fail'], a: 1,
      why: '끝내지 못한 일이 마음을 강하게 붙잡는다는 내용이므로 ‘끝나지 않은 것의 끌어당기는 힘’이 알맞다. 웨이터는 예시일 뿐이고, ④는 미완성의 유용함을 말한 글과 방향이 다르다.' },
  ],
});
ENG.add('h1', '독해: 빈칸 추론', {
  sub: '재진술과 연결어로 빈칸 채우기',
  kw: ['빈칸 추론', '재진술', 'paraphrase', '연결어', 'However', 'In other words', '오답 함정'],
  sections: [
    { h: '빈칸은 글의 핵심이다',
      p: `빈칸 추론 문제의 빈칸에는 대개 <span class="hl">글의 주제나 결론</span>이 들어간다. 글쓴이는 중요한 말을 여러 번 바꿔 말하므로, 빈칸과 <b>같은 내용을 다른 말로 표현한 문장(재진술)</b>을 지문에서 찾으면 답이 보인다. 빈칸의 위치에 따라 단서를 찾는 곳이 달라진다.`,
      table: { head: ['빈칸 위치', '빈칸의 성격', '단서를 찾는 곳'],
        rows: [['첫 부분', '주제문', '뒤에 오는 예시·근거를 묶어 일반화'],
               ['중간', '앞뒤를 잇는 내용', '바로 앞뒤 문장과 연결어'],
               ['마지막', '결론·요약', '앞 전체 내용 + Therefore, In short']] },
      ex: [[`Familiarity is not the same as memory. In other words, knowing a page well is different from remembering it.`, '익숙함은 기억과 같지 않다. 다시 말해, 어떤 쪽이 눈에 익은 것은 그것을 기억하는 것과 다르다.']] },
    { h: '빈칸 추론 풀이 순서',
      p: `보기부터 읽으면 그럴듯한 오답에 끌려가기 쉽다. <b>빈칸 문장을 먼저</b> 읽고 무엇이 들어갈지 방향을 잡은 뒤, 지문에서 근거를 찾아 <b>스스로 답을 예상</b>하고 나서 보기를 본다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일'],
        rows: [['1', '빈칸 문장을 읽고 들어갈 말의 성격(긍정/부정, 원인/결과)을 정한다.'],
               ['2', '첫 2~3문장에서 글의 소재와 흐름을 잡는다.'],
               ['3', '빈칸과 같은 뜻을 다른 말로 쓴 문장(재진술)을 찾는다.'],
               ['4', '보기를 보기 전에 우리말로 답을 예상해 본다.'],
               ['5', '보기를 넣어 앞뒤 문장과 자연스럽게 이어지는지 확인한다.']] },
      tip: '예시가 나오면 예시들의 공통점이 빈칸의 답인 경우가 많다.' },
    { h: '연결어로 방향 잡기',
      p: `빈칸 앞뒤의 <span class="hl">연결어</span>는 빈칸에 들어갈 내용이 앞 내용과 같은 방향인지 반대 방향인지 알려 준다.`,
      table: { head: ['관계', '연결어', '빈칸에 오는 내용'],
        rows: [['역접', 'However, But, Yet, In contrast, On the other hand', '앞과 반대되는 내용'],
               ['인과', 'Therefore, Thus, As a result, So', '앞 내용의 결과'],
               ['예시', 'For example, For instance', '앞 일반 진술의 구체적인 예'],
               ['재진술', 'In other words, That is, In short', '앞과 같은 내용을 다른 말로'],
               ['첨가·유사', 'In addition, Moreover, Similarly, Likewise', '앞과 같은 방향의 내용']] },
      ex: [[`Bananas are sweet. In contrast, lemons are sour.`, '바나나는 달다. 대조적으로 레몬은 시다.'],
           [`He missed the bus. As a result, he was late for school.`, '그는 버스를 놓쳤다. 그 결과 학교에 지각했다.']] },
    { h: '오답 함정 피하기',
      p: `빈칸 문제의 오답은 <b>그럴듯해 보이도록</b> 만들어진다. 정답은 지문에 근거가 있는 것이고, 오답은 근거가 없거나 방향이 반대인 것이다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정', '설명'],
        rows: [['지문 단어 재활용', '지문에 나온 단어가 들어 있지만 빈칸 자리의 뜻과 다르다.'],
               ['반대 방향', '통념(글쓴이가 반박하는 생각) 쪽의 내용이다.'],
               ['부분만 맞음', '예시 하나에만 해당하고 글 전체를 설명하지 못한다.'],
               ['상식·배경지식', '옳은 말이지만 지문에 근거가 없다.']] },
      warn: '통념-반박 구조의 글에서 앞부분 통념을 정답으로 고르는 실수가 가장 많다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: 'I studied hard for the test. <span class="blank"></span>, I failed it.', o: ['Therefore', 'However', 'For example', 'In addition'], a: 1,
      why: '열심히 공부했지만 떨어졌다는 반대 내용이 이어지므로 역접의 However가 알맞다.' },
    { lv: 1, q: 'Some animals are active at night and sleep during the day. <span class="blank"></span>, owls hunt in the dark and rest when the sun is up.', o: ['However', 'As a result', 'For example', 'Instead'], a: 2,
      why: '부엉이는 앞 문장(밤에 활동하는 동물)의 구체적인 예이므로 For example이 알맞다.' },
    { lv: 1, q: 'Tom is an early bird. <span class="blank"></span>, he likes to get up early in the morning.', o: ['In other words', 'However', 'Otherwise', 'As a result'], a: 0,
      why: '뒤 문장은 early bird(일찍 일어나는 사람)를 쉽게 다시 말한 것이므로 In other words(다시 말해)가 알맞다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Many students prepare for tests by reading their textbooks again and again. This feels productive, because the material becomes more familiar each time. However, familiarity is not the same as memory. Research shows that a much more effective method is to <span class="blank"></span>. When students close the book and try to write down or explain what they have learned, their brains must work to pull the information out. This effort strengthens the memory, making it easier to find the information later during an exam. It may feel harder and slower than rereading, but that difficulty is exactly what makes it work. In short, the best way to put knowledge into your head is to practice getting it out.</div>`,
      o: ['read the textbook in a quiet place', 'highlight the most important sentences', 'study with friends in a group', 'recall it without looking at the book'], a: 3,
      why: '빈칸 뒤 문장(책을 덮고 써 보거나 설명해 보기)과 마지막 문장(꺼내는 연습)이 재진술이다. 조용한 곳에서 읽기는 반박당한 다시 읽기 방법이다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">For a long time, people imagined a forest as a place of fierce competition, where each tree fought its neighbors for sunlight and water. Recent studies, however, reveal a surprising picture of <span class="blank"></span>. Beneath the ground, the roots of trees are connected by thin threads of fungi, forming a vast underground network. Through this network, older and taller trees can send sugar and nutrients to young trees growing in the shade. When one tree is attacked by insects, it can even send chemical warning signals to others nearby, which then prepare their own defenses. Some scientists compare this system to the internet. A forest, it turns out, is less like a battlefield and more like a community.</div>`,
      o: ['competition', 'decline', 'cooperation', 'isolation'], a: 2,
      why: 'however로 통념(경쟁)을 뒤집고, 영양분과 경고 신호를 나누는 예와 ‘공동체’라는 마지막 문장이 이어지므로 협력(cooperation)이다. competition은 통념 쪽 오답이다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">In one experiment, people were asked to taste two glasses of juice and say which was better. One glass was labeled with a high price and the other with a low price. Most people said the expensive juice tasted much better, but in fact, both glasses contained exactly the same juice. Brain scans even showed more activity in pleasure-related areas when people drank the "expensive" juice. This suggests that what we experience is shaped not only by the thing itself but also by our <span class="blank"></span>. If we believe something will be good, our brain helps make it so. This is one reason why companies spend so much money on attractive packaging and high-end branding.</div>`,
      o: ['expectations', 'memories of childhood', 'sense of smell', 'daily diet'], a: 0,
      why: '같은 주스인데 비싸다고 믿으면 더 맛있게 느낀다. 빈칸 뒤 ‘좋을 거라고 믿으면’이 재진술이므로 기대(expectations)가 알맞다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We often think that creativity needs complete freedom: no rules, no deadlines, and unlimited resources. Surprisingly, the opposite is often true. When a children's author was challenged to write a whole book using only fifty different words, he produced one of his most popular stories. Poets who must follow a strict rhyme pattern often find unexpected word choices they would never have considered otherwise. Engineers working with a tiny budget invent clever solutions that wealthy companies overlook. In each case, <span class="blank"></span> forced people to stop following familiar paths and search for new ones. Rather than blocking creativity, a clear boundary can give the mind something to push against.</div>`,
      o: ['freedom', 'limits', 'success', 'teamwork'], a: 1,
      why: '50개 단어, 엄격한 운율, 적은 예산이라는 예의 공통점은 ‘제한’이다. 마지막 문장의 a clear boundary가 재진술이다. freedom은 반박당한 통념이다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Every year, huge amounts of fresh fruit and vegetables are thrown away before they even reach stores. The reason is not that they are rotten or unsafe to eat. Some carrots are twisted, some apples have small marks on their skin, and some potatoes are simply too big or too small. Because shoppers tend to choose items that look perfect, supermarkets refuse to buy produce that does not meet strict standards. In other words, much of this food is wasted only because of its <span class="blank"></span>. Recently, some companies have begun selling these "ugly" products at lower prices, and many customers have discovered that they taste just as good.</div>`,
      o: ['price', 'taste', 'nutrition', 'appearance'], a: 3,
      why: '휘어진 당근, 흠집 난 사과, 크기가 맞지 않는 감자는 모두 겉모습의 문제이다. 썩거나 위험해서가 아니라고 했고 맛도 똑같다고 했으므로 taste는 틀리다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `(A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Sharks have a reputation as dangerous man-eaters, largely thanks to movies that show them hunting swimmers. (A) <span class="blank"></span>, attacks on humans are extremely rare. Each year, only a few dozen unprovoked attacks are reported worldwide, and many of them happen because a shark mistakes a swimmer or surfer for a seal. In fact, humans are a much greater threat to sharks than sharks are to us. Tens of millions of sharks are caught every year, mainly for their fins. (B) <span class="blank"></span>, many shark species are now in danger of disappearing. This is a serious problem, because sharks help keep ocean ecosystems healthy by controlling the populations of other sea animals.</div>`,
      o: ['For example – However', 'In addition – Similarly', 'However – As a result', 'Therefore – In contrast'], a: 2,
      why: '(A) 위험하다는 평판과 반대로 공격은 드물다 → However. (B) 해마다 수천만 마리가 잡힌 결과 멸종 위기에 처했다 → As a result.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Why do some brilliant experts make poor teachers? One reason is a phenomenon called the "curse of knowledge." Once we know something well, it becomes very difficult to imagine what it was like before we knew it. An expert pianist cannot remember the confusion of first reading music, and a math professor may find it hard to see why a student is stuck on a basic step. As a result, experts often skip steps that seem obvious to them, use technical terms without explaining them, and move too quickly. Their knowledge, which should help them teach, actually gets in the way. Good teachers, therefore, are not simply those who know the most, but those who can <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`,
      o: ['remember how it feels not to know', 'use the most advanced vocabulary', 'cover the material as quickly as possible', 'show students how much they know'], a: 0,
      why: '전문가가 잘 못 가르치는 이유는 모르던 때를 떠올리지 못하기 때문이므로, 좋은 교사는 모르는 기분을 기억할 수 있는 사람이다. 어려운 용어와 빠른 진도는 문제점으로 나온 내용이다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When learning a new skill, such as a piano piece or a dance routine, many people believe that practicing without stopping is the fastest way to improve. However, studies suggest that progress often happens when we are <span class="blank"></span>. In one experiment, people learning a simple typing task showed the greatest improvement not during practice itself but during the short rest periods between practice sessions. During these breaks, the brain seemed to replay the movements at high speed, strengthening the memory of the skill. Sleep appears to work in a similar way, organizing what we learned during the day. So if you want to learn more efficiently, don't feel guilty about pausing. Your brain is still working, even when you are not.</div>`,
      o: ['practicing at full speed', 'watching experts perform', 'taking a break from practice', 'competing against others'], a: 2,
      why: 'However로 ‘쉬지 않고 연습하기’라는 통념을 뒤집는다. 실험(쉬는 시간에 가장 향상), 수면, pausing이 모두 쉬는 것의 재진술이다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">A map that showed every single detail of a city, including every tree, every crack in the road, and every parked car, would be almost useless. It would be so crowded with information that no one could find the way to the station. A useful map works precisely because it <span class="blank"></span>. A subway map, for example, ignores the real distances between stations and the shapes of the streets above ground, showing only the lines and the order of stops. This is all a traveler needs. The same principle applies to good explanations and summaries. Their value comes not from including everything, but from choosing carefully what to show and what to ignore.</div>`,
      o: ['shows the exact size of every building', 'leaves out most of the details', 'is updated every single day', 'is drawn by professional artists'], a: 1,
      why: '모든 것을 담은 지도는 쓸모없고, 지하철 노선도는 실제 거리와 길 모양을 무시한다. 즉 유용한 지도는 세부를 생략하기 때문에 쓸모가 있다. ①은 반대 방향이다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Imagine you are visiting a new city and looking for a place to eat. You see two restaurants side by side. One is full of customers, with people waiting outside, while the other is completely empty. Most people would choose the crowded one, even though they know nothing about either restaurant. When we are uncertain about what to do, we tend to assume that others know something we don't. Psychologists call this "social proof." It explains why online stores display the number of people who have bought a product and why some comedy shows add recorded laughter. In situations of doubt, our decisions are strongly guided by <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`,
      o: ['the advice of experts', 'our own past experience', 'the price of the choice', 'the behavior of other people'], a: 3,
      why: '붐비는 식당, 구매자 수, 녹음된 웃음소리는 모두 다른 사람들의 행동을 보고 결정하는 예이다. 전문가의 조언이나 자신의 경험은 지문에 근거가 없다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When a new song comes out, you may not like it much at first. But after hearing it on the radio, in stores, and in your friends' videos, you might find yourself humming along and even enjoying it. This is not a coincidence. Psychologists have found that people tend to develop a preference for things simply because they are familiar with them. In one study, students were shown photos of strangers' faces, some many times and others only once. Later, they rated the faces they had seen more often as more likable, even though they could not remember seeing them before. It appears that <span class="blank"></span> alone can make us feel more positive toward something, which is exactly why advertisers show the same ads again and again.</div>`,
      o: ['repeated exposure', 'careful analysis', 'personal choice', 'strong emotion'], a: 0,
      why: '노래를 여러 번 듣기, 얼굴 사진을 여러 번 보기, 같은 광고 반복이 모두 ‘반복 노출’이다. 사진을 본 것조차 기억하지 못했으므로 신중한 분석이나 선택은 아니다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Anyone who has learned a language, an instrument, or a sport knows the frustration of a plateau. For weeks, you practice hard, yet you seem to be stuck at the same level. Many people quit at this point, believing that their effort is no longer paying off. But progress rarely moves in a straight line. Think of ice slowly warming in a cold room. As the temperature rises from minus five degrees to minus one, nothing seems to happen. Then, at zero, the ice suddenly begins to melt. The energy was not wasted; it was building up all along. The same is true of learning: during a plateau, improvement is often <span class="blank"></span> rather than absent, waiting to appear all at once.</div>`,
      o: ['impossible', 'hidden', 'temporary', 'unnecessary'], a: 1,
      why: '얼음의 비유처럼 겉으로 변화가 없어 보여도 에너지는 쌓이고 있다. 정체기의 향상은 ‘없는’ 것이 아니라 ‘숨어 있는’ 것이다. impossible은 포기하는 사람들의 생각(통념)이다.' },
  ],
});
ENG.add('h1', '독해: 흐름과 무관한 문장·요약문', {
  sub: '글의 초점을 잡아 흐름 판단하고 요약하기',
  kw: ['무관한 문장', '요약문', '글의 초점', '같은 소재 다른 초점', 'paraphrase', '(A)(B)'],
  sections: [
    { h: '흐름과 무관한 문장이란',
      p: `무관한 문장 문제의 정답은 대개 <span class="hl">소재는 같지만 초점이 다른</span> 문장이다. 소재가 아예 다른 문장은 쉽게 보이지만, 같은 단어(바나나, 잠, 산호 등)를 쓰면서 글이 말하려는 방향에서 벗어난 문장은 놓치기 쉽다. 먼저 첫 문장(주제문)에서 <b>소재 + 초점</b>을 정해야 한다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정 유형', '특징', '예 (주제: 잠자는 동안 뇌가 하는 일)'],
        rows: [['같은 소재, 다른 초점', '핵심 단어는 같지만 글이 말하는 방향과 다름', '잠이 잘 오게 하는 음악'],
               ['그럴듯한 상식', '사실이지만 글의 흐름에 필요 없는 정보', '사람은 인생의 3분의 1을 잔다'],
               ['연결어로 위장', 'Also, In fact 등으로 시작해 자연스러워 보임', 'Also, many people snore.']] },
      ex: [[`Sleep helps the brain store memories.`, '잠은 뇌가 기억을 저장하도록 돕는다. (소재: 잠 / 초점: 뇌의 기억 저장)']] },
    { h: '무관한 문장 풀이 순서',
      p: `무관한 문장은 하나씩 따로 보면 모두 맞는 말이다. 그래서 <b>앞뒤 문장과의 연결</b>을 보아야 한다. 의심 가는 문장을 빼고 읽었을 때 흐름이 더 자연스러우면 그것이 정답이다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일'],
        rows: [['1', '첫 문장(주제문)에서 소재와 초점을 정한다.'],
               ['2', '①~④ 각 문장이 그 초점을 뒷받침하는지 확인한다.'],
               ['3', '대명사·연결어가 앞 문장과 이어지는지 본다.'],
               ['4', '의심 가는 문장을 빼고 앞뒤 문장을 이어 읽는다.'],
               ['5', '빼고 나서 흐름이 더 매끄러우면 정답으로 고른다.']] },
      warn: '지문에 반복된 단어가 들어 있다고 관련 있는 문장은 아니다. 단어가 아니라 초점을 비교한다.' },
    { h: '요약문 풀이 순서',
      p: `요약문 문제는 <span class="hl">요약문을 먼저</span> 읽는 것이 핵심이다. 요약문은 지문의 주제문을 짧게 바꿔 쓴 것이므로, (A)(B)에 들어갈 말의 방향(증가/감소, 긍정/부정)을 먼저 정하고 지문에서 근거를 찾는다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일'],
        rows: [['1', '요약문을 먼저 읽고 글의 소재와 (A)(B)의 역할을 파악한다.'],
               ['2', '지문에서 실험·예시의 결과와 결론 문장을 찾는다.'],
               ['3', '(A)(B)의 근거가 되는 지문 표현에 표시한다.'],
               ['4', '그 표현을 다른 말로 바꾼(paraphrase) 보기를 고른다.'],
               ['5', '(A)(B) 둘 다 맞는지 확인한다. 하나만 맞는 보기가 함정이다.']] },
      tip: '실험 글은 ‘무엇을 바꿨더니 → 결과가 어떻게 달라졌다’를 한 문장으로 정리하면 그것이 요약문이다.' },
    { h: '바꿔 쓰기(paraphrase) 표현',
      p: `요약문의 (A)(B)에는 지문에 나온 단어가 그대로 나오지 않는다. 같은 뜻을 <b>다른 단어</b>로 바꾼 것을 알아보아야 한다.`,
      table: { head: ['지문 표현', '요약문 표현', '뜻'],
        rows: [['kept trying, did not give up', 'persistent', '끈기 있는'],
               ['without thinking, did not notice', 'automatic, unaware', '무의식적인'],
               ['ate less, dropped by half', 'reduce, decrease', '줄이다'],
               ['made it easier to see', 'visible, noticeable', '눈에 띄는'],
               ['more and more cluttered', 'complexity', '복잡함'],
               ['does not occur to us', 'overlook', '간과하다']] },
      ex: [[`They gave up quickly. → They were less persistent.`, '그들은 빨리 포기했다. → 그들은 덜 끈기 있었다.']] },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: '다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<br>Dogs are good companions for elderly people. ① They give their owners a reason to walk every day. ② Caring for a dog also helps owners feel needed. ③ Cats, however, sleep up to sixteen hours a day. ④ In addition, a dog\'s company can reduce feelings of loneliness.', o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 2,
      why: '노인에게 개가 좋은 친구가 된다는 글인데, ③은 고양이의 수면 시간 이야기로 초점에서 벗어난다.' },
    { lv: 1, q: '다음 문장과 뜻이 가장 가까운 것은?<br>Students who sleep well tend to remember more of what they learn.', o: ['Good sleep can improve memory.', 'Learning makes students sleepy.', 'Memory has nothing to do with sleep.', 'Students should study late at night.'], a: 0,
      why: '잠을 잘 자는 학생이 배운 것을 더 많이 기억한다는 뜻이므로 ‘좋은 잠이 기억력을 높인다’로 바꿔 쓸 수 있다.' },
    { lv: 1, q: '요약문 문제를 풀 때 가장 먼저 하면 좋은 것은?', o: ['지문의 첫 문장부터 꼼꼼히 해석한다.', '요약문을 먼저 읽고 (A)(B)에 들어갈 말의 방향을 잡는다.', '보기에 나온 단어의 뜻을 모두 외운다.', '지문의 마지막 문장만 읽고 답을 고른다.'], a: 1,
      why: '요약문은 글의 핵심을 줄인 것이므로 먼저 읽으면 무엇을 찾으며 읽어야 하는지 알 수 있다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<div class="passage">Many cities around the world are turning old railways into public parks. When trains stop using a track, the land is often left empty and becomes a place for trash. ① By planting gardens and building walking paths, cities can give this space a new life. ② These parks offer residents a safe place to walk, jog, and meet their neighbors. ③ High-speed trains can now travel at more than 300 kilometers per hour. ④ They also attract tourists, which helps local shops and restaurants grow. In this way, a forgotten piece of land can become one of the most loved places in a city.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 2,
      why: '버려진 철길을 공원으로 바꾸는 이점에 관한 글이다. ③은 ‘기차’라는 소재만 같고 고속 열차의 속도 이야기로 초점이 다르다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Researchers asked two groups of students to solve a set of difficult puzzles. Each student worked alone in a quiet room and could stop at any time. Before starting, the first group was told that the puzzles measured their natural intelligence. The second group was told that the puzzles were a chance to practice and learn new strategies. Although the puzzles were identical, the results were quite different. Students in the second group spent more time on the hard puzzles and tried more approaches. Students in the first group gave up more quickly, especially after their first failure. They seemed worried that a mistake would show they were not smart.</div>↓<div class="passage">Students who saw the task as a chance to (A) <span class="blank"></span> were more (B) <span class="blank"></span> than those who saw it as a test of ability.</div>`,
      o: ['(A) learn … (B) persistent', '(A) compete … (B) persistent', '(A) learn … (B) anxious', '(A) rest … (B) careless'], a: 0,
      why: '배우고 연습할 기회라고 들은 학생들이 더 오래 시도했다(more time, tried more) → learn, persistent. 걱정한(anxious) 쪽은 지능 검사라고 들은 집단이다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<div class="passage">Bananas are one of the most popular fruits in the world, but the bananas we eat today face a serious threat. ① Almost all bananas sold in stores belong to a single variety called the Cavendish. ② Because these plants are genetically identical, a disease that kills one plant can easily spread to all of them. ③ In fact, a fungus that attacks Cavendish plants is already spreading across farms in Asia, Africa, and Latin America. ④ Bananas are also rich in potassium, which helps keep the heart healthy. To protect the future of the banana, scientists are now working to develop new varieties that can resist the disease.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 3,
      why: '바나나가 한 품종뿐이라 질병에 취약하다는 위협에 관한 글이다. ④는 바나나의 영양소 이야기로, also로 자연스러워 보이지만 초점에서 벗어난다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">In a study, researchers placed a bowl of candy either on office workers' desks or two meters away from them. When the candy was on their desks, workers ate an average of nine pieces a day. When it was just two meters away, they ate only about four. Interestingly, the workers did not feel that walking two meters was much of an effort, and most of them did not even notice that they were eating less. The small distance simply gave them a moment to ask themselves whether they really wanted the candy. Many automatic choices disappeared when that moment existed.</div>↓<div class="passage">Even a (A) <span class="blank"></span> change in the environment can reduce (B) <span class="blank"></span> behavior by giving people time to think.</div>`,
      o: ['(A) costly … (B) careful', '(A) small … (B) planned', '(A) slight … (B) automatic', '(A) sudden … (B) social'], a: 2,
      why: '2미터라는 작은(slight) 거리 변화가 무의식적인(automatic) 사탕 먹기를 줄였다. ②는 (A)는 맞지만 (B) planned가 지문과 반대이다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<div class="passage">Most people think of sleep as a time when the brain simply shuts down and rests. In reality, the sleeping brain is very busy. ① Some people find it easier to fall asleep while listening to calm music or the sound of rain. ② During deep sleep, the brain sorts through the day's experiences and decides which memories to keep. ③ It also clears away waste products that build up in the brain while we are awake. ④ Without this nightly cleaning, these waste products could harm brain cells over time. That is why a good night's sleep is not a luxury but a basic need for a healthy brain.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 0,
      why: '잠자는 동안 뇌가 바쁘게 일한다는 글이다. ①은 잠이라는 소재는 같지만 잠드는 방법에 관한 것이라 초점이 다르다. ③의 It은 ②의 the brain을 받는다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Why do people often keep watching a boring movie after paying for the ticket, or finish a meal they don't enjoy at an expensive restaurant? The answer lies in what economists call the "sunk cost effect." Once we have invested money, time, or effort in something, we feel that quitting would mean wasting that investment. However, the money is already spent and cannot be recovered whether we stay or leave. The only real question is whether continuing will make us happier from now on. By focusing on what is already lost, people often end up losing even more: in this case, two hours of their evening.</div>↓<div class="passage">People tend to (A) <span class="blank"></span> an unsatisfying activity because of what they have already spent, even though past costs should not (B) <span class="blank"></span> their present decisions.</div>`,
      o: ['(A) quit … (B) affect', '(A) avoid … (B) change', '(A) continue … (B) support', '(A) continue … (B) affect'], a: 3,
      why: '이미 쓴 돈 때문에 지루한 영화를 계속 본다(continue). 그러나 되찾을 수 없는 과거 비용은 지금의 결정에 영향을 주면(affect) 안 된다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<div class="passage">In the past, scientists believed that the brain could not change much after childhood. Today, we know this is far from true. ① The brain constantly reorganizes itself in response to new experiences, an ability called neuroplasticity. ② For instance, taxi drivers who must memorize thousands of city streets have been found to have a larger memory-related area in the brain than most people. ③ Similarly, people who learn to juggle show changes in brain regions related to movement after just a few months of practice. ④ The human brain uses about 20 percent of the body's energy, even though it makes up only 2 percent of body weight. These findings suggest that it is never too late to learn, since our brains keep changing throughout life.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 3,
      why: '뇌는 경험에 따라 평생 바뀐다(신경 가소성)는 글이다. ④는 뇌의 에너지 사용량이라는 사실이지만 ‘변화’라는 초점과 관계없다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Many places have tried to reduce litter by putting up signs that say "Don't throw trash on the ground." Surprisingly, these signs often have little effect. A group of researchers tried a different approach at a city park. Instead of warning signs, they painted green footprints on the ground leading to the nearest trash can. Visitors naturally followed the footprints, and the amount of litter dropped by nearly half. The footprints did not tell anyone what to do or not to do. They simply made the right action easier and more noticeable. This kind of gentle push, which guides behavior without forcing it, is known as a "nudge."</div>↓<div class="passage">Rather than (A) <span class="blank"></span> people, a nudge changes behavior by making the desired action more (B) <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`,
      o: ['(A) guiding … (B) difficult', '(A) warning … (B) visible', '(A) paying … (B) expensive', '(A) warning … (B) optional'], a: 1,
      why: '경고 표지판(warning) 대신 발자국으로 올바른 행동을 더 쉽고 눈에 띄게(noticeable → visible) 만들었다. guiding은 넛지 자체가 하는 일이므로 (A)에 맞지 않는다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<div class="passage">Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they are home to about a quarter of all marine species. ① Fish use the reefs as places to feed, hide from predators, and raise their young. ② The reefs also protect coastlines by breaking the force of waves during storms. ③ Some tourists collect pieces of coral as souvenirs, although many countries ban this practice. ④ In addition, many medicines have been developed from chemicals found in reef animals. For all these reasons, the loss of coral reefs due to rising ocean temperatures would be a disaster not only for marine life but also for humans.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 2,
      why: '산호초가 바다 생물과 사람에게 주는 혜택(서식지, 해안 보호, 의약품)을 나열한 글이다. ③은 산호라는 소재만 같고 관광객의 기념품 수집 이야기라 흐름에서 벗어난다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">In many workplaces, managers believe that offering bonuses is the surest way to improve performance. For simple, repetitive tasks, this is often true: the more workers are paid per item, the more items they produce. However, a series of experiments has revealed a surprising pattern for tasks that require creative thinking. When participants were offered large rewards for solving puzzles that needed a fresh perspective, they actually performed worse than those who were offered small rewards or none at all. The researchers explained that a big reward narrows our focus. We concentrate so hard on the prize that we fail to notice unusual solutions lying at the edges of the problem. Rewards, it seems, help us run faster on a straight road but make it harder to find a new path.</div>↓<div class="passage">While rewards can increase performance on (A) <span class="blank"></span> tasks, they may (B) <span class="blank"></span> performance on tasks that demand creativity.</div>`,
      o: ['(A) creative … (B) improve', '(A) routine … (B) improve', '(A) difficult … (B) double', '(A) routine … (B) lower'], a: 3,
      why: '보상은 단순 반복 작업(simple, repetitive → routine)의 성과는 높이지만, 창의적 과제에서는 오히려 성과를 떨어뜨렸다(performed worse → lower).' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글에서 전체 흐름과 관계 <b>없는</b> 문장은?<div class="passage">For centuries, sailors crossed oceans without satellites or modern instruments. How did they find their way? Polynesian navigators, for example, read the natural world with remarkable skill. ① They observed the rising and setting points of particular stars to keep their direction at night. ② They also studied the patterns of ocean waves, which bounce off islands and change shape in ways that experienced sailors could feel through the bottom of the canoe. ③ Many modern ships, by contrast, are so large that passengers rarely feel the motion of the sea at all. ④ Even birds offered clues, since certain species fly out to sea in the morning and return to land at dusk. By combining these signs, navigators could locate tiny islands across thousands of kilometers of open water.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 2,
      why: '폴리네시아 항해자들이 별·파도·새를 이용해 길을 찾았다는 글이다. ③은 by contrast로 ②의 ‘파도를 느낀다’와 이어지는 듯 위장했지만 현대 선박 승객 이야기로 초점이 다르다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 내용을 한 문장으로 요약할 때, (A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When people face a problem, their first instinct is usually to add something: a new rule, an extra feature, another step. In one study, participants were asked to improve a structure made of toy blocks so that it could hold a weight. The easiest solution was to remove a single block, yet most participants added new blocks instead, even when adding cost them money. Similar results appeared when people were asked to improve a travel plan or an essay; they rarely considered taking anything out. The researchers concluded that subtraction simply does not occur to us as often as addition. This may explain why our schedules, homes, and organizations tend to become more and more cluttered over time.</div>↓<div class="passage">People tend to (A) <span class="blank"></span> the option of removing things when solving problems, which leads to unnecessary (B) <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`,
      o: ['(A) prefer … (B) simplicity', '(A) overlook … (B) complexity', '(A) overlook … (B) savings', '(A) consider … (B) complexity'], a: 1,
      why: '빼는 방법은 잘 떠오르지 않는다(does not occur to us → overlook). 그래서 일정·집·조직이 점점 어수선해진다(cluttered → complexity).' },
  ],
});
ENG.add('h1', '독해: 도표·안내문·내용 일치', {
  sub: '숫자·조건·사실을 하나씩 대조하기',
  kw: ['도표', '안내문', '내용 일치', 'twice as', 'percentage point', '비교 표현', '대조 확인'],
  sections: [
    { h: '도표 문제: 비교 표현 익히기',
      p: `도표 문제는 영어 설명 ①~④를 표의 숫자와 <span class="hl">하나씩 대조</span>하는 문제이다. 어려운 것은 계산이 아니라 <b>비교 표현</b>을 정확히 읽는 것이다. 특히 배수, 순위, 퍼센트포인트 표현에 주의한다.`,
      table: { head: ['표현', '뜻', '예'],
        rows: [['twice as ~ as / more than twice', 'A는 B의 두 배 / 두 배 넘게', '40은 20의 twice, 45는 more than twice'],
               ['more than half / less than one-fifth', '절반이 넘는 / 5분의 1이 안 되는', '8은 46의 less than one-fifth'],
               ['the second highest', '두 번째로 높은', '1위 다음 숫자'],
               ['A, followed by B', 'A가 1위, 그다음이 B', 'reading, followed by volunteering'],
               ['percentage point(s)', '퍼센트 숫자 자체의 차이', '20% → 30%: 10 percentage points 증가'],
               ['the gap between A and B', 'A와 B의 차이', '38과 21의 gap은 17']] },
      ex: [[`The number of girls was twice as large as that of boys.`, '여학생 수는 남학생 수의 두 배였다.'],
           [`Its share fell by 17 percentage points.`, '그 비율은 17퍼센트포인트 떨어졌다.']] },
    { h: '도표 풀이 순서',
      p: `도표 설명의 첫 문장은 도표의 <b>제목과 같은 내용</b>이므로 무엇을 비교하는 도표인지만 확인하고 넘어간다. 그다음 ①~④를 하나씩 읽으며 <b>대상·연도·숫자·비교 방향</b>을 표와 맞춘다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일'],
        rows: [['1', '도표의 제목·단위(%, 시간, 명)·항목을 확인한다.'],
               ['2', '각 문장의 대상(누가/무엇이)과 시점(어느 해)을 찾는다.'],
               ['3', '비교 표현(more, less, twice, the highest)을 표시한다.'],
               ['4', '표에서 해당 숫자를 찾아 직접 계산·비교한다.'],
               ['5', '한 문장 안의 두 내용(앞부분·뒷부분)이 모두 맞는지 확인한다.']] },
      warn: '문장의 앞부분만 맞고 뒷부분(“which was also true in 2014” 등)이 틀린 경우가 많다. 끝까지 확인한다.' },
    { h: '안내문·내용 일치 풀이 순서',
      p: `안내문과 내용 일치 문제는 <span class="hl">보기를 먼저</span> 읽고 지문에서 해당 부분을 찾아 확인한다. 보기는 대개 지문 순서대로 나오므로 차례로 대조하면 된다. 내 상식이 아니라 <b>지문에 쓰인 사실</b>만으로 판단한다.`,
      table: { head: ['구분', '주로 확인할 것', '자주 나오는 표현'],
        rows: [['안내문', '날짜·시간·장소·대상·비용·신청 방법·주의 사항', 'up to(최대), by(~까지), provided(제공됨), in case of rain'],
               ['인물 글', '태어난 곳·시기, 계기, 업적, 연도', 'At the age of, Later, In 1928'],
               ['동식물·장소 글', '사는 곳, 특징, 먹이, 수명, 현재 상태', 'only, Unlike, Despite, rare']] } },
    { h: '오답 함정 피하기',
      p: `일치·불일치 문제의 오답은 지문 내용을 <b>살짝 바꾼</b> 것이다. 숫자 하나, 단어 하나가 바뀌었는지 끝까지 확인한다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정', '예'],
        rows: [['숫자·날짜 바꾸기', '11월 6일 마감 → 11월 20일까지 제출'],
               ['비교 방향 뒤집기', 'less than ↔ more than, 증가 ↔ 감소'],
               ['범위 넓히기', '13~19세 → 20세 이하 누구나'],
               ['조건 빠뜨리기', '드문 해에만 → 해마다'],
               ['상식으로 판단', '지문에 없는데 “그럴 것 같다”로 고르기']] },
      tip: '불일치 문제는 ‘일치’ 보기를 하나씩 지워 가면 실수가 줄어든다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: 'The number of girls was <b>twice as large as</b> that of boys. 남학생이 30명이라면 여학생은 몇 명인가?', o: ['15명', '30명', '60명', '90명'], a: 2,
      why: 'twice as large as는 ‘두 배’라는 뜻이다. 30 × 2 = 60명.' },
    { lv: 1, q: '어떤 비율이 20%에서 30%로 올랐다. 영어로 바르게 표현한 것은?', o: ['It increased by 10 percentage points.', 'It increased by 10 percent.', 'It doubled.', 'It decreased by 10 percentage points.'], a: 0,
      why: '퍼센트 숫자 자체의 차이(30 - 20 = 10)는 percentage points로 말한다. 20에서 30은 50% 증가이므로 ‘10 percent 증가’는 틀리고, 두 배도 아니다.' },
    { lv: 1, q: `다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Summer Reading Camp</b><br>• Dates: July 22–26<br>• Time: 10:00 a.m. – 12:00 p.m.<br>• For: students aged 10 to 15<br>• Fee: free (bring your own lunch)<br>• Sign up at the front desk by July 15.</div>`,
      o: ['7월 15일까지 신청해야 한다.', '캠프는 오후에 열린다.', '참가비를 내야 한다.', '점심이 제공된다.'], a: 0,
      why: '‘by July 15’는 7월 15일까지라는 뜻이다. 시간은 오전 10시~낮 12시, 참가비는 무료이고 점심은 각자 가져와야 한다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 도표의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Favorite After-School Activities of Riverside High School Students (%)</b><table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Boys</th><th>Girls</th></tr><tr><td>Sports</td><td>38</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming</td><td>27</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>9</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Music</td><td>14</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Volunteering</td><td>12</td><td>23</td></tr></table>The table above shows the favorite after-school activities of boys and girls at Riverside High School. ① For boys, sports was the most popular activity, chosen by 38 percent. ② Among girls, reading was the most popular activity, followed by volunteering. ③ The percentage of boys who chose gaming was more than twice that of girls who chose gaming. ④ Music was chosen by a higher percentage of boys than girls.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 3,
      why: '음악은 남학생 14%, 여학생 20%로 여학생이 더 높다. ③은 27%가 12%의 두 배(24%)를 넘으므로 맞다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Greenville Community Garden Day</b><br>Join us to make our neighborhood greener!<br>• When: Saturday, April 12, 9:00 a.m. – 1:00 p.m.<br>• Where: Greenville Park (next to the library)<br>• Activities: planting flowers, building birdhouses, making compost<br>• Who can join: anyone aged 8 and older (children under 12 must come with an adult)<br>• Gloves and tools will be provided. Please wear comfortable shoes.<br>• Free lunch for all volunteers!<br>※ The event will be canceled in case of rain.</div>`,
      o: ['행사는 도서관 옆 공원에서 열린다.', '12세 미만 어린이는 어른과 함께 와야 한다.', '장갑과 도구는 각자 준비해야 한다.', '비가 오면 행사가 취소된다.'], a: 2,
      why: '‘Gloves and tools will be provided’는 장갑과 도구를 나누어 준다는 뜻이다. 각자 준비할 것은 편한 신발이다.' },
    { lv: 2, q: `Clara Benson에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage">Clara Benson was born in 1902 in a small farming town in Canada. As a child, she spent hours watching birds near her family's farm and drawing them in a notebook. Her family could not afford to send her to college, so she worked at a local post office while teaching herself biology from library books. In 1928, a professor who saw her bird drawings at a town fair invited her to join a research trip to the Arctic. Her detailed drawings from that trip were later published in a book that became popular with both scientists and the public. Benson continued to study birds for the rest of her life and founded a small nature school for local children in 1951.</div>`,
      o: ['그녀는 대학에서 생물학을 전공했다.', '1928년에 북극 연구 여행에 초대받았다.', '그녀의 그림책은 과학자들에게만 인기를 얻었다.', '1951년에 대학 교수가 되었다.'], a: 1,
      why: '1928년에 교수의 초대로 북극 연구 여행에 참여했다. 대학에 가지 못하고 독학했으며, 책은 과학자와 일반인 모두에게 인기였고, 1951년에는 자연 학교를 세웠다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 도표의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Average Daily Screen Time of Teenagers by Purpose (hours)</b><table><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>2020</th><th>2024</th></tr><tr><td>Watching videos</td><td>2.1</td><td>2.6</td></tr><tr><td>Social media</td><td>1.4</td><td>1.9</td></tr><tr><td>Gaming</td><td>1.2</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>Studying online</td><td>0.8</td><td>0.9</td></tr><tr><td>Messaging</td><td>0.6</td><td>0.5</td></tr></table>The table above shows how many hours teenagers spent on screens each day for different purposes in 2020 and 2024. ① In both years, watching videos took up the most time among the five purposes. ② The time spent on social media increased by 0.5 hours from 2020 to 2024. ③ Gaming was the only purpose whose time decreased between the two years. ④ In 2024, the time spent studying online was less than half of the time spent on social media.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 2,
      why: '게임(1.2 → 1.0)뿐 아니라 메시지(0.6 → 0.5)도 줄었으므로 ‘유일하게 줄었다’는 틀리다. ④는 0.9가 1.9의 절반(0.95)보다 작으므로 맞다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `Westbrook High School Science Fair에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Westbrook High School Science Fair</b><br>Show off your scientific creativity!<br>• Date: Friday, November 20<br>• Place: School Gym<br>• Who: All Westbrook students (individuals or teams of up to 3)<br>• How to enter: Submit your project plan to Mr. Lee (Room 204) by November 6.<br>• Each project must include a poster and a working model or an experiment.<br>• Prizes: 1st place – a tablet PC / 2nd place – a science book set<br>• Projects will be judged by our science teachers and a guest scientist from Westbrook University.</div>`,
      o: ['팀은 최대 4명까지 구성할 수 있다.', '프로젝트 계획서는 11월 20일까지 제출해야 한다.', '포스터 없이 모형만 제출해도 된다.', '외부 과학자도 심사에 참여한다.'], a: 3,
      why: '대학에서 온 초청 과학자(a guest scientist)가 심사에 참여한다. 팀은 최대 3명, 계획서는 11월 6일까지, 포스터와 모형(또는 실험)을 모두 갖춰야 한다.' },
    { lv: 3, q: `Arctic tern에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage">The Arctic tern is a small seabird that weighs only about 100 grams, yet it makes the longest migration of any animal on Earth. Every year, it flies from its breeding grounds in the Arctic to the coast of Antarctica and back again, a round trip that can be as long as 70,000 kilometers. Because it follows the summer from one end of the world to the other, the Arctic tern sees more daylight than any other creature. It feeds mainly on small fish, which it catches by diving into the water from the air. Arctic terns can live for more than 30 years, so over a lifetime, one bird may fly a distance equal to about three round trips to the Moon.</div>`,
      o: ['몸무게가 약 100그램에 불과하다.', '주로 물 위에 떠서 곤충을 잡아먹는다.', '해마다 북극과 남극 사이를 왕복한다.', '30년 넘게 살 수 있다.'], a: 1,
      why: '주로 작은 물고기를 먹고, 공중에서 물속으로 뛰어들어 잡는다. 곤충이나 물 위에 떠서 사냥한다는 내용은 없다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 도표의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Share of Electricity Generation by Source in Country A (%)</b><table><tr><th>Source</th><th>2014</th><th>2024</th></tr><tr><td>Coal</td><td>45</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>Natural gas</td><td>22</td><td>26</td></tr><tr><td>Nuclear</td><td>20</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Solar</td><td>3</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Wind</td><td>4</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>6</td><td>4</td></tr></table>The table above shows the share of electricity generated from different sources in Country A in 2014 and 2024. ① Coal was the largest source in both years, although its share fell by 17 percentage points. ② The share of solar power in 2024 was more than five times that in 2014. ③ In 2024, natural gas produced a larger share of electricity than nuclear power, which was also true in 2014. ④ The combined share of solar and wind power in 2024 was greater than that of nuclear power.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 1,
      why: '태양광은 3%에서 14%로 약 4.7배이므로 ‘다섯 배 넘게’는 틀리다. ①은 45 - 28 = 17, ④는 14 + 9 = 23 > 19로 맞다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `City Library Book Swap Event에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>City Library Book Swap Event</b><br>Give a book, take a book!<br>• Date & Time: Sunday, June 7, 1:00 p.m. – 5:00 p.m.<br>• Location: City Library, 2nd-floor lobby<br>• How it works: Bring up to 5 used books in good condition. For each book you bring, you will receive one ticket. Use your tickets to choose books from the swap tables.<br>• Not accepted: textbooks, magazines, and damaged books<br>• Books left over after the event will be donated to local schools.<br>• No registration is needed. Just drop by!</div>`,
      o: ['최대 5권까지 책을 가져올 수 있다.', '가져온 책 한 권당 티켓 한 장을 받는다.', '잡지는 교환 대상으로 받지 않는다.', '참가하려면 미리 등록해야 한다.'], a: 3,
      why: '‘No registration is needed’이므로 미리 등록할 필요가 없다. 나머지는 up to 5, one ticket for each book, Not accepted: magazines와 일치한다.' },
    { lv: 4, q: `Mira Okafor에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage">Mira Okafor grew up in Lagos, Nigeria, where frequent power cuts often left her family's house in darkness. At the age of fourteen, she built a small lamp powered by a bicycle wheel so that she and her younger brothers could study at night. After finishing high school, she won a scholarship to study engineering in the United Kingdom. There, she began developing a low-cost solar lantern that could be charged in six hours of sunlight and last an entire night. Instead of selling the lanterns for profit, she started a nonprofit organization in 2015 that trained local women to build and repair them. By 2023, more than 200,000 lanterns had been distributed across West Africa.</div>`,
      o: ['열네 살 때 태양광 랜턴을 처음 만들었다.', '장학금을 받아 미국에서 공학을 공부했다.', '랜턴은 여섯 시간 충전으로 밤새 쓸 수 있었다.', '랜턴을 판매한 수익으로 회사를 세웠다.'], a: 2,
      why: '햇빛에 6시간 충전하면 밤새 쓸 수 있는 랜턴이었다. 14세에는 자전거 바퀴로 움직이는 램프를 만들었고, 공부한 곳은 영국이며, 이익을 위해 팔지 않고 비영리 단체를 세웠다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 도표의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Main Reasons for Using Public Libraries by Age Group (%)</b><table><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Ages 13–19</th><th>Ages 20–39</th><th>Ages 40+</th></tr><tr><td>Borrowing books</td><td>31</td><td>38</td><td>52</td></tr><tr><td>Studying</td><td>46</td><td>22</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Using computers / Wi-Fi</td><td>12</td><td>18</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Attending programs</td><td>6</td><td>14</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>5</td><td>8</td><td>6</td></tr></table>The table above shows the main reasons people of different age groups used public libraries. ① For the 20–39 age group, attending programs was a more common reason than using computers or Wi-Fi. ② Among teenagers, studying was the most common reason, accounting for nearly half of the responses. ③ Borrowing books was the most common reason for both the 20–39 and the 40+ age groups. ④ The percentage of people aged 40 and older who used libraries for studying was less than one-fifth of that of teenagers.</div>`,
      o: ['①', '②', '③', '④'], a: 0,
      why: '20~39세에서 프로그램 참여는 14%, 컴퓨터·와이파이 이용은 18%이므로 ①이 틀리다. ④는 46의 5분의 1이 9.2이고 8은 그보다 작으므로 맞다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `Harbor City Youth Photography Contest에 관한 다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Harbor City Youth Photography Contest</b><br>Theme: "Small Moments in My Neighborhood"<br>• Who can enter: Harbor City residents aged 13 to 19<br>• Entry period: September 1–30<br>• How to enter: Upload up to two photos, each with a short title, to our website. Each photo must have been taken within the past year.<br>• Rules: You may adjust brightness and color, but adding or removing objects is not allowed. Photos taken with smartphones are welcome.<br>• Prizes: Grand Prize (1 winner) – a digital camera / Excellence Prize (3 winners) – a set of photography books<br>• Winners will be announced on October 15, and all winning photos will be displayed at City Hall for one month.</div>`,
      o: ['하버 시에 사는 20세 이하 누구나 참가할 수 있다.', '사진은 한 사람당 세 장까지 올릴 수 있다.', '수상작은 시청에서 일주일 동안 전시된다.', '스마트폰으로 찍은 사진도 출품할 수 있다.'], a: 3,
      why: '‘Photos taken with smartphones are welcome’과 일치한다. 참가 대상은 13~19세로 범위가 좁고, 사진은 두 장까지, 전시는 한 달 동안이다.' },
    { lv: 5, q: `the Atacama Desert에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage">The Atacama Desert in northern Chile is often described as the driest non-polar place on Earth. Some weather stations there have recorded almost no rain for many years in a row. The desert is so dry, and its soil so similar to that of Mars, that space agencies have used it to test equipment for missions to the red planet. Despite the harsh conditions, life has not completely disappeared. Tiny bacteria survive beneath the surface of rocks, and in rare years when heavy rain does fall, the desert suddenly bursts into color as millions of flowers bloom. This event, known locally as the "flowering desert," attracts visitors from around the world. The clear, dry air also makes the Atacama one of the best places on Earth for observing stars, and several major telescopes have been built there.</div>`,
      o: ['일부 관측소에서는 여러 해 동안 거의 비가 기록되지 않았다.', '해마다 봄이 되면 사막 전체에 꽃이 핀다.', '화성 탐사 장비를 시험하는 장소로 쓰였다.', '맑고 건조한 공기 덕분에 큰 망원경들이 세워졌다.'], a: 1,
      why: '꽃은 ‘큰비가 내리는 드문 해(in rare years)’에만 핀다. ‘해마다’는 조건을 빠뜨린 오답이다.' },
  ],
});
